One of the most useful advanced App Router patterns combines **Parallel Routes** and **Intercepting Routes** to create a modal that has a real shareable URL.

### Desired Behavior

From a photo gallery:

```text
/gallery
```

clicking photo 123 should show:

```text
Gallery remains visible
+
Photo 123 modal opens
```

with a navigable URL for the photo.

But opening that photo URL directly should display:

```text
Full photo page
```

rather than the modal.

### Normal Photo Route

```text
app/
  photo/
    [id]/
      page.tsx
```

This gives the standalone page.

### Add a Modal Slot

Conceptually:

```text
app/
  @modal/
    default.tsx
```

The layout receives the modal slot:

```tsx
export default function RootLayout({
  children,
  modal
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  modal: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        {children}
        {modal}
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

### Intercept the Photo Route

Inside the modal slot, create the appropriate interception route:

```text
@modal/
  (.)photo/
    [id]/
      page.tsx
```

The exact matcher depends on where the slot and destination route are positioned in the route tree.

### Intercepted Page

```tsx
import Modal from '@/components/Modal';
import Photo from '@/components/Photo';

export default async function PhotoModal({ params }) {
  const { id } = await params;

  return (
    <Modal>
      <Photo id={id} />
    </Modal>
  );
}
```

### default.tsx

The slot should render nothing when no modal is active:

```tsx
export default function Default() {
  return null;
}
```

### Closing the Modal

A modal can integrate with browser navigation:

```tsx
'use client';

import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';

export default function Modal({ children }) {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => router.back()}>
        Close
      </button>

      {children}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Why This Pattern Is Better Than a Local Boolean

A normal state-only modal:

```javascript
const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
```

cannot naturally provide all of these together:

```text
Shareable URL
Browser Back closes modal
Browser Forward reopens modal
Direct URL renders full page
Page context remains during soft navigation
```

### Interview-Ready Answer

URL-based Next.js modals combine a Parallel Route slot with an Intercepting Route. Client-side navigation renders the destination inside the modal slot while preserving the surrounding page, whereas a direct visit or refresh renders the destination's normal full page. This provides deep linking and natural browser Back/Forward behavior.

With Cache Components enabled, the `'use cache'` directive tells Next.js that the result of an async function or component can be reused instead of recomputed for every request.

It can be applied at several scopes.

### Function-Level Cache

```typescript
export async function getCourses() {
  'use cache';

  return db.course.findMany({
    where: {
      published: true
    }
  });
}
```

Repeated calls with the same inputs can reuse the cached result.

### Component-Level Cache

```tsx
export async function PopularCourses() {
  'use cache';

  const courses = await getPopularCourses();

  return <CourseList courses={courses} />;
}
```

Now the rendered component result can be cached.

### File-Level Cache

```typescript
'use cache';

export async function getCourses() {
  // ...
}

export async function getCategories() {
  // ...
}
```

A file-level directive marks its async function exports as cacheable.

### Inputs Become Part of the Cache Key

Suppose:

```typescript
export async function getArticle(slug: string) {
  'use cache';

  return db.article.findUnique({
    where: { slug }
  });
}
```

Then conceptually:

```text
getArticle('react-hooks')
→ cache entry A

getArticle('nextjs-routing')
→ cache entry B
```

Arguments help Next.js distinguish cached results.

### Closed-Over Values Also Matter

Values captured from an outer scope can also contribute to the cached computation's identity.

### Do Not Read Request APIs Directly in Normal use cache

Normal cached scopes should not directly depend on request-specific APIs such as:

```text
cookies()
headers()
```

because shared cached results should not accidentally depend on one user's request context.

Instead, read request data outside and pass the required value explicitly when appropriate.

### Cache the Smallest Useful Unit

Instead of caching an entire page merely because one expensive query is reusable:

```text
Cache the expensive data/component
```

and leave truly request-specific content outside it.

### Interview-Ready Answer

`'use cache'` can be placed on an async function, component, or file to make its result reusable when Cache Components is enabled. Function arguments and relevant captured values participate in the cache identity. Prefer caching the smallest useful scope and keep normal request APIs such as cookies and headers outside shared cache scopes.

A difficult caching problem appears when part of a page depends on request-specific information such as cookies or headers, while another expensive operation could still be reused.

The key principle is:

```text
Read request-specific data outside the shared cache scope
→ pass only the required value into cached work
```

### Problem

Suppose a profile depends on a session cookie:

```typescript
const session = (await cookies()).get('session');
```

You should not casually place the request cookie access inside a normal shared `'use cache'` function.

### Better Structure

```tsx
import { cookies } from 'next/headers';
import { Suspense } from 'react';

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<ProfileSkeleton />}>
      <Profile />
    </Suspense>
  );
}

async function Profile() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies();
  const sessionId = cookieStore.get('session')?.value;

  if (!sessionId) {
    return <p>Not signed in</p>;
  }

  return <CachedProfile sessionId={sessionId} />;
}
```

Cached component:

```tsx
async function CachedProfile({
  sessionId
}: {
  sessionId: string;
}) {
  'use cache';

  const profile = await getProfile(sessionId);

  return <ProfileView profile={profile} />;
}
```

### Why This Works

The runtime request API stays outside the shared cache scope.

The derived value:

```text
sessionId
```

is passed explicitly as an input.

That input can become part of the cache identity.

### Be Careful with Personal Data

Just because something can technically be used as a cache key does not mean sensitive personalized output should always be stored in a broad shared cache.

Evaluate:

```text
Privacy
Cardinality
Cache lifetime
Invalidation
Security
Storage cost
```

before caching personalized data.

### Runtime Data Needs Suspense with Cache Components

Request APIs such as:

```text
cookies()
headers()
searchParams
```

require request-time work.

With Cache Components, such sections should be isolated behind appropriate Suspense boundaries when they cannot be part of the prerendered shell.

### connection()

If a component needs to run specifically at request time but does not naturally use a request API, Next.js provides:

```typescript
import { connection } from 'next/server';

await connection();
```

This explicitly defers subsequent work until a real request exists.

### Interview-Ready Answer

With Cache Components, keep request-specific APIs such as `cookies()` and `headers()` outside normal shared `'use cache'` scopes. Read the runtime value in a request-time component, pass only the required value into cached work when appropriate, and isolate request-time sections with Suspense. Use `connection()` when you need to intentionally defer work to request time without reading a request API.
